    text: "The family is the most fundamental social unit in every culture. A typical nuclear family consists of parents — a mother and father — and their children: sons and daughters. Siblings, whether brothers or sisters, grow up together and share formative experiences. In many households, the parents are a married couple — a husband and wife — though modern families come in many forms, including single-parent families. Family roles have evolved significantly. Traditionally, the father was the breadwinner while the mother was the homemaker, but today both parents often share professional and domestic responsibilities equally. Children transition from being infants cared for entirely by their parents to becoming independent adults who eventually start their own families. Family bonds are reinforced through daily routines — shared meals, homework help, bedtime stories — and through celebrations like birthdays, holidays, and family reunions. Communication within a family shapes children's development: supportive parents raise confident children, while a nurturing home environment builds emotional security. Understanding family vocabulary is essential because family is one of the first and most frequent topics in any language.",
